{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791652173,"nonce":"tpL831DVh1eXPzwBsgxFkYKZqQw=","title":"Lua: each load() keeps its joined module's HIR for as long as the process runs","message":"Every chunk `load` compiles is joined into the running program (TieredRuntime::join_typed_program) and compiled at the baseline tier. The backend then keeps the joined module whole: TieredBackend.loaded holds an Arc of it, and so does the FunctionEntry of each of its functions, because a JIT rebuild recompiles every loaded module and lookups (handlers, error-flag globals, global slots by name) walk them. Nothing frees it, so each load keeps its lowered and optimised HIR for good.\n\nMeasured on the Mac (release, light build, 45e070db) with a script that loads `local a, b = ... ; local t = {a = a, b = b}; return t.a + t.b + i` N times, then waits, read with vmmap and malloc_history -callTree on the paused process:\n\n- heap growth about 62 KB per load (100 loads 27.1 MB peak footprint, 800 loads 70.4 MB); JIT memory per load is under 2 KB since 45e070db.\n- of that, about 50 KB is the module's HIR: values and blocks maps from lowering and phi_prune's sweep, the instruction vectors boxes::run_function rebuilds, the HirGlobal and HirFunction maps. The rest is Cranelift declarations and relocations (about 6 KB) and small maps.\n\nOfficial files that load a lot pay for it in peak footprint: constructs.lua joins 6775 chunks (539 MB peak), gc.lua 5008, locals.lua 1285 (348 MB), literals.lua 129.\n\nFix: once a joined module's code is installed, keep its functions as shells (signature, attributes and shell_refs, no body) and the bodies in a split image encoded in memory with the ids as they are (bytecode::encode_module_in_place), set as each entry's body_source. A rebuild decodes them back to recompile the module whole; the interpreter's body source serves them to a host call_raw that interprets a joined function; the effect walk behind missing_stateful_handler decodes the bodies it reads. A module that is current_module or has lazy functions keeps its HIR.","files":null}]}